WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.936
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Chelsea Handler.

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:04.203
And you're gonna shoot this
as a special,

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:06.106
which is very exciting.
-Yes, in Nashville,

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:08.340
June 10th at the Ryman.
We have two shows.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.043
You can get tickets
for the second show

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:12.278
at chelseahandler.com.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.413
And Jo Koy's directing
my special.

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:16.282
-That's really exciting.
And I think it's very important

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.250
where you pick
to shoot a special.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.719
Obviously, you've been
almost everywhere.

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:23.189
You have a good sense for what
audiences respond to you best.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.590
So, is a Nashville place
that you've always had,

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.792
historically, great shows?
-Yeah, I love Nashville.

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:28.627
That's just a great vibe,
good energy.

00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:29.929
You know,
all the Southern cities

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:32.798
that have those, like,
you know, fun, like, pockets

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:34.934
of progressiveness
-- you know, what I mean? --

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.102
where people are a little bit --
I mean, I just did a show

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:39.205
in Birmingham, Alabama.
-I've done Birmingham.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.307
It's great.
-It was one of the best shows

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.676
I've ever had, and
I was dreading going there.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:45.578
I was like, "Oh, I'm gonna
get shot," you know?

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.513
-Yeah.
You had to cancel some shows,

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.883
during your tours,
for health reasons.

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.250
-Oh, yeah.
Well, thank you

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.418
for bringing that up, Seth.
-You're welcome.

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:54.086
-I --
-And I remember you told me

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.788
this was -- you said
it was confidential.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.324
That's right. Keep going. Yeah.
-Uh, yeah.

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:01.160
I had this heart --
it's called a cardiomyopathy.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.330
And I guess a lot of women
between the ages of 40 and 50

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.798
have been experiencing this
lately.

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:08.301
But I went onstage, and
I had this chest tightening.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.102
And then, after this,
after my show,

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:12.305
I had a lot of friends there,
agents, managers,

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.941
blah, blah, blah.
Everyone went out to a bar,

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:16.842
and it was the first time
I've ever said no to that.

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:18.377
-Wow.
-I didn't feel right.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:19.812
I was like, "Something's off."

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.747
And I went into the hospital
the next day,

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:25.184
and they're like, "Oh, you've
had a cardiomyopathy,"

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:28.421
which just means, like, your
heart feels like it got punched.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:31.290
And it's recoverable.
90% of people recover,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.360
which I've already recovered.
But, yeah, it was scary.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.295
I was like, "Oh, my God,
all my hard living

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:38.431
is finally catching up to me."
I'm like, "This is it.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.600
I'm gonna pass away tonight."
And my sister was like,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.902
"Oh, my God, did you have
a heart attack?"

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.270
I'm like, "I think I had
a heart attack."

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.705
The doctor's like, "You did not
have a heart attack."

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.541
So, it's a cardiomyopathy.
You can Google it.

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:51.244
[ Laughter ]
-After you Google "Jake."

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.579
-Yeah, yeah, after you
Google my nephew.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:54.814
But you know what?

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.048
The doctor said something
really funny to me.

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:58.050
Well, not --
More poignant than funny.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.187
He was -- He said to me,
He said, "Isn't your whole --"

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.622
He goes, "I'm not really
familiar with you.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.924
I mean, I know who you are,
but isn't your whole schtick,

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.426
like, not to take yourself
too seriously?"

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:10.162
And I was like, "Yeah."
And he goes, "Well, maybe

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:13.466
you should take your own advice,
'cause it's stress-induced."

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.768
And I was like, "Wow."
I was like, "You're right.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.837
Maybe I do need to
take my own advice.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.538
I am taking myself
too seriously."

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:22.074
-Well, that's very --
It's nice when a doctor

00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:23.743
can impart wisdom like that.
That's a wonderful thing.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.911
-I mean, it is their job.
-Yeah, that's true.

00:02:25.911 --> 00:02:27.647
-So, yeah.
-That's great.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.548
Yeah, let's not put him
on a pedestal, doctor --

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.250
doctor who saved
Chelsea's life.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.651
[ Laughs ]
-Yeah, I wish

00:02:32.651 --> 00:02:34.420
I could remember his name.
I'd give him a shout-out, too,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.522
but, you know,
just focus on Jake.

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:37.990
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.993
-I know you take medicine
very seriously,

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:42.929
except I also know for a fact

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.464
that you've taken
some of your dog's medicine.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.933
-I -- Well...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:49.168
Wow. This is like
you read my diary.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:50.669
-I think you put this
in a book, though.

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:54.106
-Well, my dog and I
were on a trip in Spain.

00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:56.408
-Yeah.
-And my dog

00:02:56.408 --> 00:02:58.244
was a great traveler.
He's no longer with us,

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.079
not because of what I did
to him, but...

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:04.083
He was a great traveler.
And I decided he needed to

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.619
start traveling internationally
as a reward.

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:09.889
And so we went to Spain
with my family.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:13.026
And I have a sister-in-law
that's pretty annoying.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.827
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.997
And I was -- And she's very --
Just in the sense

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.898
that she's very overprotective
over my nephews.

00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:21.934
Like, she puts sunblock
all over their faces.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.936
Like, she'd put it in their
mouths if she could.

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:26.238
And they're, like,
18-year-old boys.

00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:27.840
Like, it's embarrassing
at a certain point.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.742
It's like, "Get off of them.
You're embarrassing me."

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:32.745
And anyway, we were sailing,

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.580
and one day I just
couldn't take it anymore.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.849
I was like, "I have to jump
off the boat," you know?

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:40.687
And I swam to shore --
not unlike Julia Roberts

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.020
in "Sleeping With the Enemy."
-Yeah.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.556
-And I swam to shore,
and I went in my house,

00:03:44.556 --> 00:03:46.158
and my Aunt was standing there,
and she goes,

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:47.426
"What's the matter?
You can't take --"

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:48.760
you know, my sister-in-law --
"anymore?"

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.196
I go, "No, she's
driving me crazy."

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:52.398
And she said, "Well, you know,
this doggy medicine

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.835
that you got for the dog is
basically like doggy Xanax,

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.904
which is real Xanax."
-Yeah.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:59.672
-And I was like,
"Great, give it to me."

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.608
Like, "That'll
take the edge off."

00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:05.010
So I took those, you know,
electively

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.379
for the rest of the trip.
-Okay.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.581
-Just to temper my, you know,
temper.

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.817
I have a bad --
I have a bad reaction

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.351
to people that annoy me.
-Yeah.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:15.521
-And but then,
when we got on the trip back,

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:18.023
I realized I had taken
all of my dog's medicine

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:19.758
to get him back to the States."

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:21.093
-'Cause he needs it
for a flight.

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:22.561
-He needed it for the flight.
-Gotcha.

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:25.164
-So the flight was
a real hot mess.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.366
[ Laughter ]
-And when I woke up --

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:29.902
Well, I was woken up by one of
the flight attendants,

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.104
who told me that my dog
was running around

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:35.641
the first-class area,
and that I needed to wake up.

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:39.312
And it was -- It was
a [Bleep] show at the end.

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.147
I was convinced,
as soon as we landed

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:42.915
and got back to LAX,
I was like,

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.116
"Oh, they're gonna arrest me,"

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:45.885
like, "Animal Services
are coming to get me,

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:48.387
and they're gonna arrest me,

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:49.856
and they're gonna
take my dog away."

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.324
And so they didn't.
Everything worked out fine.

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:54.027
I mean, I've never flown
that airline again.

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.262
[ Laughter ]
Not because it was bad.

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:58.363
I just thought, they've met
enough of me.

00:04:58.363 --> 00:05:00.899
You know what I mean?
I made my impression --

00:05:00.899 --> 00:05:01.968
-You elected out.
-Yes, yes.

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.602
-You made another
elective choice.

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:05.003
-So I haven't been
to Spain since.

00:05:05.003 --> 00:05:06.772
-Oh, that's great.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.208
So you both took the --
you both are making yourself

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.344
not fly the airline
or even go back to the country

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:12.411
after what you did.
-Yeah, I just figured

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:14.013
I should give the whole region
a break.

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:15.214
[ Laughter ]
You know what I mean?

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.282
-That's very kind of you.
-Once you kind of

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.516
make that kind of impression,
it's good

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.285
to just take some time out
and then go back

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:20.419
when you can
make a new impression.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:21.987
-Yeah, that's great.
Thank you.

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:23.188
It is always so lovely
to see you.

00:05:23.188 --> 00:05:25.557
Congratulations on the show,
and best of luck

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:27.293
at the special taping.
Chelsea Handler.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.829
Tickets to her
"Vaccinated and Horny" tour

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.397
available at
chelseahandler.com.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:32.899
We'll be right back
with Martha Stewart.

